      "narration": "Every single thing running on a Linux machine is a process with a numeric ID, and there are two tools for looking at them that answer different questions. Ps takes a snapshot: this is what was running at the instant you asked. The flags a u x are almost always what you want, and honestly most people have simply memorized ps aux as one word. Top is the opposite — a live feed that refreshes every couple of seconds, sorted by whatever is consuming the most CPU, which makes it the right tool when the question is what is making this machine slow right now. Inside top, press capital M to sort by memory and capital P for CPU, and q to quit. If htop is installed, use it; it is the same idea with colours, mouse support and a far more readable layout. The structural thing to understand is that processes form a tree, not a list. Every process records the PID of the parent that started it. Follow those links all the way up and you arrive at PID one, which is systemd, started by the kernel at boot. Everything else on the machine is a descendant of that one process. That tree matters, because killing a parent affects its children, which is the next slide.",
