      "narration": "The kill command is badly named. What it actually does is send a signal, and killing is only one of the things signals do. The default signal is SIGTERM, number fifteen, and it means please shut down cleanly. A well-written program catches it and does the right things: finishes the request it is handling, flushes its buffers to disk, closes its database connections, and exits. SIGKILL, number nine, is completely different. It is not delivered to the program at all — the kernel simply destroys the process where it stands. It cannot be caught, blocked, or ignored, and crucially, the program gets no chance to clean up. Half-written files and corrupt databases come from dash nine. So the discipline is: always send plain kill first, wait a few seconds, and only escalate to dash nine if the process genuinely will not go. And notice SIGHUP, which most long-running daemons interpret as reload your configuration without restarting — that is how you apply a config change to a web server without dropping a single connection. One last thing that will save you confusion: if a process ignores even dash nine, it is probably in D state, stuck waiting on disk or network I/O the kernel cannot interrupt. That usually means hardware trouble or a hung network mount, not a stubborn program.",
